  "query_preview": "..Mostly dry and stable conditions with near normal\ntemperatures will persist for the region through Saturday. The \none exception will be across portions of southern and southeastern\nUtah where they remain on the periphery of tropical moisture that\nis currently spread across the eastern Rockies and western \nplains. Forcing for these storms today will be limited which will \nkeep most of the isolated convection across the higher terrain, \nbut rainfall rates could be heavy at times. Friday seems to have a\nbit more coverage in precipitation across eastern/southeastern \nUtah as a shortwave moves through the area and provides more \nforcing for storms.\n\nGuidance continues to trend towards the wetter solution for the \ntrough that will move through early next week. Now 50% of members \nare marrying this trough with tropical moisture from Hurricane \nPolo as it makes landfall in western Mexico and treks to the east.\nThe other 50% of members keep a bulk of this moisture across New \nMexico and Colorado. If the wetter solution plays out then the \nrisk for flash flooding will increase across the area given that \nsoils are moist from the past several weeks of monsoon moisture. \nThe coolest air of the season will accompany the airmass that \nmoves in behind a cold front that is ushered in by the trough.",
